// A multi-byte character in a literal. The automaton runs on bytes, so the é of "café" is two transitions and the
// token covers five bytes for four characters. The trace reports byte offsets throughout, which is what every backend
// has to agree on regardless of how its own language counts string positions.
//
// NAME only reaches "caf" of "café", so the longer match is the one which walks through the multi-byte character.
//
// The parser is not what this case is about, so the grammar is the single empty production the file format requires.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    CAFE: "café";
    NAME: /[a-z]+/;
}

@parser {
    tokens
        : @empty
        ;
}
